  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nCentral Idea: We employ a multi-resolution optimization combined with sparse representation to approximate the optimal step function. We start with the parent solution and then refine it at higher resolutions using a genetic algorithm with spatial penalization for discontinuities. \n\nKey Steps:\n- Maintain three grid resolutions: 45, 90, and 180 points\n- At each resolution, represent h as a piecewise constant function with an actual number of discontinuities d\n- Optimize the function triple: [avg_coeff, locations, discontinuity locations] \n- Maximize the separation (locations) and penalize complexity (avg_coeff)\n- Transition adaptive resolution strategy to balance exploration vs. exploitation\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nFor grid size N (from {45, 90, 180})\ndx = 2.0/N\n\nVariables:\n1. avg_coeff: Average coefficient for constant intervals\n2. locations: Number of discontinuity points (singularities)\n3. discontinuities: Positions of active discontinuities (in [0,2])\n\nbut note:\navg_coeff < 2 locations and discontinuities must cover [0,2]\n\nObjective:\n5.5 Penalty_formulation_for_dissipation_and_non-local_interference\n\nConstraints:\n- For each grid, enforce sum(h_i) = N/2 exactly\n- 0 <= h(x) <= 1 for all x \u2208 [0,2]\n\nImplementation:\n- Use iterative overresolution to transfer gradients from coarse to fine grids\n- Preserve feasibility by: 1. Grid interpolation 2.Constraint projection \n- The optimization function will be convex only at discontinuity boundaries, so use variable projection and smoothed max enforcement\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nThe coder should:\n\na) Setup the parent validation (n=45, dx=2/45) using initial_h_values\n\nb) Define the core optimization algorithm for each resolution:\n\n   i) Partition [0,2] into segments based on a (fine) resolution grid\n   \n   ii) Genetic algorithm structure:\n        - Chromosome regulations (using codon/word encoding)\n        - Population: (avg_coeff, discontinuity_count, locations)\n        - Fitness function: max_{k} [overlap integral score] with multi-peak detection\n   \n   iii) Crossover and mutation operators for the triple representation\n   \n   iv) Repair mechanisms: feasible if avg_coeff[0]*(x2-x1) + ... = integral=1 and within [0,1]\n\nc) Implement grid transfer:\n\n   1) Coarse to fine (doubling):\n        - Transfer solution by resampling the parent peaks at higher resolution\n        \n   2) Fine to coarse:\n        - Project solution onto lower resolution lattice\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n   Genetic Algorithm Parameters:\n   - Population size: 50\n   - Generations: 200 per resolution\n   - Crossover rate: 0.8\n   - Mutation rate: 0.1\n   - Tournament selection with elitism\n\n   Fitness assessment for overlfiled boundaries:\n   - Evaluate correlation at M alternating points (to avoid local max)\n\n   Feature of innovation:\n   - Probability of introducing new discontinuities: 0.05\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\n   Per resolution optimization time ~ O(n_points*N. logN)\n\n   Steps for budget management:\n   - For resolution 45: budget 3s\n   - For resolution 90: budget 5s\n   - For resolution 180: budget 10s\n   \n   Use a cooling schedule for population diversity\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n   - Ensure grid solution for N points must satisfy: 0<= h_i <=1, sum(h)=N/2\n   - Re-evaluate final solution using the correlation method\n   - Format: (h_values, c5_value, n_points)\n\n   Validation steps:\n   - Double-check grid boundaries and discontinuity support\n   - Compute C5 with np.correlate\n   - Verify integral condition",
